 q</a> · <a href="results/enrichment_summary.json">Denominators, label sources and test assumptions</a></p><p><a href="#3">Real image-to-cluster examples above</a></p><p class="finding">Organ and scale associations appear in both ordinary and overlapping clusters; overlap changes membership counts and compositions. Read effect sizes together with support and component-level q values.</p><p class="limit"><b>Limit.</b> FDR refers to independent-component presence associations, not the image composition itself. Components must be independent/exchangeable; scene/site correlation may remain. Scale labels are provisional BioImages/Gemma operational labels; organ is saved BioImages metadata; family uses preserved legacy taxonomy (including Aceraceae/Hippocastanaceae). These are exploratory associations, not confirmatory biological findings.</p></section><section id="5"><small>EXPERIMENT 5</small><h2>Fixed-case decision and activation exploration</h2><p><b>Question.</b> Do path differences change memberships, and does occluding a hotspot reduce the cluster-proximity score?</p><p><b>Method.</b> Fresh FP32 CPU run on all12 fixed original photographs, plus reuse of archived three-path stability experiment. Same frozen BioCLIP checkpoint, original unnormalized20 centers, crop and normalized output. Real final attention-block ln_1 activation Grad-CAM excludes CLS and uses16×16 patch tokens. Fixed score = negative squared Euclidean distance to original center. Batch1 forward, gradient and occlusion calls share one recorded path; MHA fastpath disabled. High-response top52 patches compared with5 seeded random masks of exactly52 patches.</p><figure><a href="results/activation_fp32/occlusion_all12.png"><img loading="lazy" src="results/activation_fp32/occlusion_all12.png"></a><figcaption>Both masks replace exactly52/256 patches with mean RGB. All individual random scores are saved. Larger positive drops move farther from the fixed original centroid; SD is descriptive, not a confidence