 7</small><h2>Image inversion through frozen BioCLIP</h2><p><b>Question.</b> Does matching an embedding recover recognizable leaf, bark or fruit appearance?</p><p><b>Method.</b> One fixed original image per organ, two random RGB starts (seeds42/43). Freeze all632M visual parameters. Optimize64×64 RGB pixels, bilinearly upsample224, CLIP normalization, FP32/default SDPA CPU batch1 with MHA fastpath disabled. Real target is recomputed through the same encoder path. Adam .05 for12 updates; loss = 1−cosine + .01 squared adjacent-pixel TV. Pilot estimated about4 seconds/update before expansion.</p><figure><a href="results/inversion/feature_distance.png"><img loading="lazy" src="results/inversion/feature_distance.png"></a><figcaption>All six real optimization trajectories, including non-monotonic steps. Lower means closer to the same-path target embedding.</figcaption></figure><table border="1" class="dataframe">
